<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sikh conquest of Kashmir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1819">1819</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghan-sikh-wars">Afghan–Sikh Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> took place in 1819, when the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> took the valley from its Afghan rulers. Kashmir became a valuable province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a>, famed for its shawl industry. It was later lost after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-sikh-war">First Anglo-Sikh War</a> and transferred to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulab-singh">Gulab Singh</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sikh-conquest-of-kashmir.jpg" alt="Sikh conquest of Kashmir" width="320" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See description · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Sikh-ruled_Kashmir,_given_by_General_Claude-Auguste_Court_to_Victor_Jacquemont,_1831.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a>'s interest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> was older than the campaign. In 1813 he cooperated with Wazir Fateh Khan in an invasion meant to remove Atta Muhammad Khan, and in 1814 his own expedition failed amid heavy rain, cold, short supplies and Afghan resistance in the passes; he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> in August. The conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a> in 1818 gave him new resources, while Muhammad Azim Khan left Kashmir for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, entrusted it to his brother Jabbar Khan and took much of the experienced Afghan force with him. Bir Bal Dhar, a Kashmiri revenue official at Ranjit Singh's court, pressed for an invasion and left his son at Lahore as a pledge.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Misr Diwan Chand was made commander-in-chief and led the main force with Akali Phula Singh, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharak-singh">Kharak Singh</a> commanded another column, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> kept a reserve; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hari-singh-nalwa">Hari Singh Nalwa</a> was among the officers. Estimates of the army range from about 12,000 to some 35,000 including non-combatants. Kharak Singh's advance force left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> on 26 February 1819, heavy guns were left at Bhimber, and by June Rajouri and Poonch were secured. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> troops destroyed the fort and plundered Rajouri, with arson, abductions and the molestation of women.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akali-movement">Akalis</a> breached and stormed the fort of Tari, the army forced the Pir Panjal Pass and won a day-long fight at Aliabad, and Jabbar Khan's entrenched army was beaten near Shopian, where he was wounded; several historians date the battle to 5 July 1819, one to 3 July. Jabbar Khan withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srinagar">Srinagar</a> was occupied, on 4 or 15 July according to different historians. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> had forbidden looting, but his troops plundered the city until Diwan Chand stopped them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Diwan Chand ruled under a military administration and received the title Zafar Jang before Diwan Moti Ram became governor with 20,000 troops. Bir Bal Dhar took the revenue farm, assessed at fifty-two to fifty-three lakh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a>, and the shawl manufacture was contracted for ten lakh a year. The Jama Masjid in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srinagar">Srinagar</a> was closed, the public azan prohibited and cow slaughter banned. Resistance continued towards Poonch and Muzaffarabad. Ranjit Singh told the British officer C. M. Wade in 1827 that the province yielded twenty-five lakh rupees a year.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sikh conquest of Kashmir” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The conquest of Multan in 1818 secured southern Punjab, and the <b>Sikh conquest of Kashmir</b> in 1819 brought the valley under Lahore's authority.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
